  "enhanced_information": "The 67-year-old man presents with low hemoglobin, elevated serum creatinine, and a peripheral blood smear showing stacks of red blood cells, which is suggestive of multiple myeloma. The serum electrophoresis revealing an abnormal protein spike supports this diagnosis, as multiple myeloma is characterized by the production of monoclonal proteins. Bence-Jones proteins, which are free light chains of immunoglobulins, are often found in the urine of patients with multiple myeloma. Therefore, the most relevant additional finding expected in this patient would be the presence of Bence-Jones proteins in the urine, confirming the diagnosis of multiple myeloma. Other options, such as smudge cells, are more indicative of chronic lymphocytic leukemia and not consistent with the findings here. Normal aging cannot fully explain the patient's symptoms, as they are more indicative of an underlying hematological disorder."
